# Auto-commit & push to staging

After meaningful implementation work in this repo (features, fixes, config, docs you were asked to add), **immediately commit and push to `staging`**. Do not wait for the user to ask. This is the default day-to-day workflow.

**Multitask exception:** When Multitask Mode (or multiple parallel agents) has an open batch, **commit locally** as usual but **defer `git push`** until the whole batch is done — see `multitask-staging-push`. Single-task sessions still push immediately after verify.

## Branch policy

- **`staging`** is the integration target — day-to-day work lands there first (see `staging-and-releases`).
- Prefer working **on `staging`**. If you are on a feature branch, finish by getting the commit(s) onto **`origin/staging`** (simplest path: checkout `staging`, merge or cherry-pick as needed, then push). Do not push day-to-day work straight to `main`.
- If both this frontend repo and Beauty-Backend-API were changed for one feature, **commit and push each repo** to its own `staging` branch.

## Commit hygiene

1. `git status`, `git diff`, and recent `git log` before committing.
2. Stage only relevant files; focused message that explains **why**.
3. On Windows PowerShell, use a here-string for the message:

```powershell
git commit -m @"
Commit message here.
"@
```

4. Then: `git push -u origin staging` (or push the updated local `staging` tracking branch).

## Safety (unchanged)

- Never commit secrets (`.env`, `prod.env` with live keys, API tokens). Use `.env.example` / `.env.staging.example` only.
- Never force-push to `main`. No hard reset / destructive git ops unless the user explicitly requests them.
- Skip commit only when there is nothing meaningful to commit (empty/WIP-only noise), or the user explicitly said not to commit this change.

## Precedence

This rule **overrides** “wait for the user to ask before committing/pushing” for normal implementation work on this project. Destructive git operations and secret handling still require caution as above.
